
.*
# CI configuration is source, not a dotfile-of-convenience: the blanket
# `.*` above had kept the entire workflow set out of the repository.
# CI is not tracked for now (owner decision, 2026-08-17): the blanket `.*`
# rule above therefore applies to .github/ as well.  Restoring CI means
# putting `!.github/` back here -- and re-tracking tests/, because
# ci.yml's `ctest -L unit` step has nothing to run without it.
# !.github/
!.gitignore
!.gitattributes
!.gitattributes

engine/src/middleware/audio/third_party
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/minimp4.h

engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/*
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/jce_build
# JCE-authored port artefacts that must live INSIDE the upstream tree because
# the build references them at those paths.  They are not upstream code and
# cannot be re-fetched: ithread_win32.c is a JCE Win32 implementation of
# libhevc's pthread-only ithread.h, and the x86 platform-macros header carries
# the _MSC_VER branch (declspec align, __nop, __popcnt) that x86 MSVC builds
# compile against -- jce_build's generic copy is only on the include path for
# Emscripten and ARM64.  Both were lost once already and had to be recovered
# from a 2024 commit; track them so a vendor-tree rebuild cannot drop them.
#
# git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded, so each
# level has to be re-admitted and then re-excluded on the way down.
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/
engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/*
# ...and then the two files themselves.  These four lines were MISSING:
# both files were force-added once and have survived only because they sit
# in the index, where ignore rules no longer apply.  Re-vendoring libhevc
# and running a plain `git add` would have dropped them a second time --
# the exact loss the paragraph above says already happened once.  x86/ needs
# its own admit/exclude pair because git will not descend into a directory
# excluded by `common/*`, so a rule naming only the file inside it never
# gets read.
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/ithread_win32.c
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/x86/
engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/x86/*
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/libhevc/common/x86/ihevc_platform_macros.h
engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/openh264/*
!engine/src/middleware/video/third_party/openh264/jce_build


_*

# ...but THREE COMMITTED files under scripting/python/jce_script/ begin with an
# underscore, because that is Python's spelling of "private module" and of
# "package entry point", and the rule above has no path anchor so it hides them
# at every depth.  Two of them are generated AND committed on purpose
# (engine/CMakeLists.txt globs sources at CONFIGURE time, so an artefact
# emitted during the build joins its target one build late); the third is the
# package's __init__.py, without which `import jce_script` finds nothing.
#
# While they are ignored, `git add <path>` refuses them BY NAME and `git add -A`
# skips them in SILENCE -- which is how a binding ships with no bindings in it.
# Worse in this shared worktree: a pathspec commit builds a temporary index
# that is incomplete for ignored files, so the next session's commit deletes
# them.  That has happened here before.
#
# This re-admits three NAMED files, not the underscore prefix:
# scripting/python/jce_script/__pycache__/ stays ignored, and
# `git check-ignore -q <path>` returning 1 for these three is what says so.
!scripting/python/jce_script/_generated.py
!scripting/python/jce_script/_generated.pyi
!scripting/python/jce_script/__init__.py

build/
out/


caged_kingdom/resources/assets/**
# The Kerr black hole lab's content layer is SOURCE, not cooked output: the
# scene, its Lua controller, kerr_lab.json (the semantic authority per the
# design's section 10.3) and the four numeric-texture LUTs.  A blanket ignore
# on assets/ made all of it invisible to git, so a git clean would have taken
# the whole demo silently.  Parent directories are re-included explicitly
# because git cannot re-include a file underneath an excluded directory.
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/black_hole/
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/black_hole/**
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/scenes/
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/scenes/black_hole_lab.scene.json
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/scripts/
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/scripts/black_hole_lab.lua
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/scripts/black_hole_lab.lua.deps.json
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/textures/
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/textures/black_hole/
!caged_kingdom/resources/assets/textures/black_hole/**
engine/resources/assets/shaders/
editor/resources/assets/fonts/
!editor/resources/assets/i18n/
!editor/resources/assets/i18n/**


raw_assets/


scripts/ZKM-VMP/

sonar-project.properties
CMakeUserPresets.json

bak/
TODO/


# `docs/*`, NOT `docs/` -- excluding the DIRECTORY makes git refuse to descend into
# it, and a `!docs/...` line below can then never match. Excluding its CONTENTS keeps
# loose files under docs/ ignored while letting the three re-includes below work.
# (The i18n block above gets away with `!editor/resources/assets/i18n/` only because
# its parent `editor/resources/assets/` is not itself excluded.)
docs/*
# ...the three subtrees that are project record rather than scratch.
# Two of these are read by CI: tools/audit/ resolves docs/architecture/
# dependency-ownership.yml and docs/audits/duplicate-implementation-inventory.yml,
# and both were UNTRACKED under the blanket rule -- the audit could not see its own
# inputs.  The rest is the spec/plan trail that every recent feature was built from.
#
# Re-including a path inside an excluded directory needs BOTH lines: git never
# descends into an excluded directory, so the directory itself must be re-included
# before a glob inside it can match.  Same two-line idiom as the i18n block above.
#
# This also closes a silent data-loss path: a file force-added under an ignored
# directory lives only in whatever index added it, so any concurrent session that
# rebuilds the shared index drops it from the next commit's tree -- observed twice.
halloween-test/

sync_*.py


agents.*
# ...but NOT AGENTS.md, which this rule was swallowing on every case-insensitive
# checkout (Windows, macOS): `agents.*` matched `AGENTS.md` at every depth, so 51 of
# the repo's 54 per-directory charters existed only on the machine that wrote them.
# A fresh clone got three. That is the same failure as `docs/` and `tools/audit/`:
# a broad rule quietly eating the project's own record. It had been patched one file
# at a time (`!/space/AGENTS.md` below, plus two force-adds) instead of at the root.
# Root AGENTS.md makes updating the nearest charter mandatory when files move — a
# rule nobody else can see is not a rule.
# AGENTS.md is NOT tracked (owner decision, 2026-08-17).  Anchored per
# name, so it matches at every depth -- there are 85 of them on disk.
# CONSEQUENCE, stated because it is not obvious: scripts/lint/check_agents_md.py
# verifies that every required AGENTS.md is IN PLACE, and its canonical list
# lives in .docs/MEMORY_INDEX.md, which is also untracked.  The gate still
# passes locally because the files are on disk; on a clean clone it would
# report all of them missing.  That is the same trade already accepted for
# tests/ and docs/, not a new one.
AGENTS.md
# Anchored: an unanchored `tests/` also swallowed engine/tests, space/tests
# and tools/tests -- the same class of bug as `space/` and `.*`. Only the
# scratch tree at the repo root is meant to be ignored.
# The whole root suite, not just scratch/ (owner decision, 2026-08-17).
# ANCHORED, for the reason the comment above already gives: an unanchored
# `tests/` swallowed engine/tests, space/tests and tools/tests.  Those are
# other trees' suites and stay tracked.
/tests/
# Sub-suites too (owner decision, 2026-08-17): tools/audit/tests/ is the
# gates' own self-test.  Anchored per directory rather than as a bare
# `tests/`, which this file records swallowing engine/tests, space/tests
# and tools/tests when it was written unanchored.
/tools/audit/tests/
# The flac is commercial copyrighted music, 77 MB, and git history is
# permanent: once pushed, removing it needs a history rewrite, not a
# revert.  The rest of caged_kingdom/ stays tracked.
/caged_kingdom/resources/assets/black_hole/*.flac
# NOT `tools/audit/`.  Ignoring the whole directory (added in 9507d1ac) left
# the architecture/licence audit suite untracked, while .github/workflows/
# ci.yml:44 still runs `python tools/audit/run_architecture_audit.py` — so on
# any clean checkout the gate step cannot find its own script.  That suite is
# what is supposed to catch contract drift like the patented-codec gating, and
# it has not been able to run since.  Ignore only its bytecode.
# tools/audit/ is NOT tracked (owner decision, 2026-08-17).  The comment below
# is kept because it records why the directory was un-ignored in the first
# place, and that reasoning is still true -- it simply lost its argument when
# CI stopped being tracked too.  The one file the ENGINE needed from here,
# cdecl.py (the repo's single C-declaration parser), moved to tools/scriptgen/
# beside its only consumers rather than being restored with the audit suite.
tools/audit/

# Sample projects (local-only, deliberately unversioned).
# Leading slash matters: an unanchored `science_lab/` also matches
# `scripts/science_lab/`, which silently swallowed the repo-side generator
# tooling for those demos.  Anchor them to the top level only.
/science_lab/
/street_demo/
/meadow_valley/
/elemental_serenity/
# game project
# caged_kingdom/

# Stellar Clean Orbit (space/) is NOT tracked (owner decision, 2026-08-17),
# like the other consumer projects above.  It was the one exception -- the
# flagship demo carrying the deterministic-simulation contract, four ctest
# gates and the parity verifier -- and those gates live inside it, so they
# leave with it.  Nothing under engine/ or tools/ referenced space/: the
# only greps that matched were "whitespace/" and "profile_space/".
# ANCHORED, because an unanchored `space/` is the bug this file already
# records twice.
/space/


